export const lpContent={
'bee':{
 title:'The Bee',one:'Stripes, a smile and the most famous sound in the garden — the bee card teaches the first and biggest garden rule: we watch, and our hands stay down.',
 seoTitle:'Bee Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Bees (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: buzz like the bee on the card, watch a flower from a safe little distance and plant the kindest garden rule — bees are watched, never grabbed.',
 intro:'This lesson starts the garden with its busiest worker: your toddler learns the word bee, makes the buzzy sound, and meets the golden rule that holds the whole set together — eyes do the visiting, hands stay down.',
 what:'A bee is a small flying insect with yellow and black stripes that visits flowers to gather pollen and make honey. Bees spend the whole day travelling from bloom to bloom — the garden’s own delivery service.',
 mean:'The bee is the loudest hello in the garden and usually the first bug a toddler can already point at. Naming it properly turns a random buzz into a friend greeted by name — and the bee carries the set’s most important rule on its stripes.',
 know:'Two things matter: bees say bzzz and visit flowers all day long, and we watch them without touching. Bees are busy workers, not pettable pets — calm eyes and down-held hands are how we say hello.',
 why:'Look-don’t-grab is easiest to learn from a bee, because the reason is built into everyday life: everyone already treats bees with respect. Your toddler practises the rule exactly where the whole family already follows it.',
 dev:'First-insect vocabulary, sound imitation (bzzz is a lifelong favourite), watching-without-touching — genuine impulse practice — and the early idea that living things have jobs to do.',
 vocab:'bee, buzz, flowers, stripes, honey, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say bee slowly, twice — then add a long buzzy bzzz together, so the word arrives with its sound.\n2) Ask your toddler to find the bee’s smile and trace its stripes with one finger — the picture study that two-year-olds do best.\n3) Make your hand a little bee and fly it around the room, touching down softly on cushions and shoulders — soft landings only, bees are careful pilots.\n4) Look out of a window at any flowers and whisper: is a bee visiting right now? Maybe, maybe not — the looking is the game.\n5) End with the rule in toddler words: bees are watch-friends. We look, we buzz, and our hands stay down by our sides.',
 say:'"Here is the bee! Bzzz... can you say bzzz? The bee wears stripes and it loves flowers. When we see a real bee, we stand still like trees and watch — no grabbing, no squealing. Bees are busy, and our job is to watch them work."',
 notice:'Say bee together and give the card a big buzzy bzzz. The bee on this card is friendly and smiling — ask your toddler to find its smile and point at the stripes. Pointing is how two-year-olds study a picture.',
 ask:'"What does the bee say?" "Can you find the stripes?" "Where does the bee go all day?" "Do our hands touch the bee, or stay down?" — answered with a buzz, a point and a solemn little nod.',
 childSay:'"Bzzz!" delivered at full volume, hand-bees circling your head at breakfast, and bees pointed out in books, on rugs, out of prams. Some toddlers will freeze into their best statue pose at the first real buzz — the rule already working.',
 real:'Bees appear wherever flowers do — window boxes, balconies, the lawn, the herb pot on the sill. Every real buzz is a free rerun: whisper bee, keep feet still, watch it work, wave it goodbye.',
 play:'Bumble landing: your toddler flies around as the bee while you hold up a cushion-flower. Touching down softly on the flower scores a cheer; bumpy crashes get a gentle reminder that careful bees make the best honey.',
 check:'Next time a bee flies past outside, watch the hands: if they stay down and the mouth whispers bee, the lesson landed — watching replaced grabbing, which was the whole point.',
 tips:'Practise the statue pose on the card first, when no bee is anywhere near — calm is rehearsed best in peacetime. Keep sessions short: one buzz, one point, one rule, done.',
 safety:'Bees sting when they feel squashed or trapped, so the looking happens from a little way back and hands stay far away. If one lands close by, still feet and a quiet voice until it lifts off — and a grown-up guides the moment, never a swatting hand.'},
'butterfly':{
 title:'Butterfly',one:'Wings painted orange and black — the butterfly card is the prettiest flap-along lesson in the garden, and the gentlest: butterflies arrive softly and leave quickly.',
 seoTitle:'Butterfly Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Butterflies (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: flutter your arms like the butterfly on the card, move slowly past real flowers and learn why butterflies are watched kindly, never caught.',
 intro:'This lesson makes the prettiest card a whole-body game: your toddler becomes a butterfly, flapping slow and gentle, then learns the watching rule — a butterfly’s wings belong to the air, not to our hands.',
 what:'A butterfly is an insect with two pairs of big painted wings that flap slowly from flower to flower. It lands to sip nectar, rests with its wings open, and flies off the moment anything moves too fast.',
 mean:'Butterflies hand a two-year-old the perfect movement word: flutter. The flap is slow, the mood is calm, and the animal is far too quick and delicate to hold — which is exactly why the lesson works on gentleness.',
 know:'Butterflies flap slowly and love flowers, and they are look-at friends: chasing only sends them away. Hands stay down; the wings are prettiest in the air where they belong.',
 why:'A creature that leaves when chased teaches cause and effect more kindly than any lecture: quick hands mean a gone butterfly, still hands mean it stays. The lesson teaches self-control with a living, flying reward.',
 dev:'Slow-and-gentle body control (the crossed-wing flap is surprisingly tricky), vocabulary for wings and flowers, watching practice at garden speed, and the earliest what-happens-if reasoning.',
 vocab:'butterfly, flutter, wings, flowers, soft, slow',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say butterfly — a big, beautiful mouthful, so say it slowly and let your toddler try the syllables.\n2) Lift both arms overhead, cross them at the wrists, and flap slowly — a butterfly’s own unhurried flutter — and let your toddler copy.\n3) Flutter around one cushion together: slow wings find flowers, fast wings scare them away. The speed is the lesson.\n4) Whisper the story: butterflies sip from flowers, rest a while, then fly off — so we tiptoe past and let them get on with it.\n5) Finish with the rule, warm and short: butterflies are look-at friends. We flap like them, we watch them, and our hands stay empty.',
 say:'"A butterfly! Big painted wings — flap, flap, slowly, slowly. Butterflies love flowers and take their time. When we see one, we walk soft and watch with our eyes. Our hands stay down, and the butterfly stays with us a little longer."',
 notice:'Say butterfly together, then make slow crossed-arm wings. The butterfly on this card has orange and black wings — ask your toddler which colour shows the most, and flap once for each colour they can find.',
 ask:'"What do the butterfly’s wings look like?" "Can you flutter slowly?" "Where does the butterfly like to rest?" "Do we catch the butterfly, or watch it?" — answered with a flap, a whisper and a proud head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Fly!" with crossed-arm flaps at the window, butterflies discovered in every second picture book, and the first real sighting met with a hush instead of a dash — the gentleness training already showing.',
 real:'Butterflies drift through gardens, parks and balconies on sunny days, resting on petals and fences. Each one is a rerun: name it, flap once, watch it sip, and let it drift off without a single grabbed handful.',
 play:'Flower to flower: scatter a few paper flowers on the floor and flutter from one to the next, resting at each with wings open — slow, calm, and the finest quiet-time game in the set.',
 check:'Ask for the butterfly move tomorrow: crossed arms and a slow flap means the word has grown wings. If your toddler whispers instead of shouting at a real one, the gentleness has landed too.',
 tips:'Flap slowly yourself — your pace sets your toddler’s. If a real butterfly startles and darts away, narrate the happy ending: it flew home. Chasing is not a failure; it is simply the next chance to practise still hands.',
 safety:'Butterfly wings are covered in tiny dust-fine scales that fingers rub off, so catching squeezes the colour right off them — and garden flowers may host other bugs too. Watching from a small distance keeps both the butterfly and the toddler clean.'},
'ladybug':{
 title:'Ladybug',one:'Tiny, round and red with dots — the ladybug card is the counting card of the garden, hiding spots to point at and a helper story inside one little beetle.',
 seoTitle:'Ladybug Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Ladybugs (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: find the ladybug’s spots on the card, count them out loud, paint a dot family and learn that this tiny garden helper stays on its leaf.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the garden’s tiniest celebrity into a pointing-and-counting game: your toddler finds the spots, counts as far as the mood holds, and hears the lovely secret that ladybugs look after the flowers.',
 what:'A ladybug is a very small, round beetle with a red shell and black spots. It crawls slowly over leaves and stems, eating tiny bugs that would otherwise nibble the garden — a small friend with a big job.',
 mean:'The ladybug matches a toddler’s own scale: little, round, red and friendly. Pointing at its spots is often a two-year-old’s very first purposeful counting — real pointing, real numbers, one tiny beetle.',
 know:'The ladybug is red with spots and crawls on leaves, and it helps the flowers by eating pests. It is a look-at friend too — small things are exactly the things fingers want to poke, so hands stay gentle and down.',
 why:'Counting spots on a card is counting without pressure: any number reached is a win, and the same beetle offers a fresh total every time. The helper story plants the first seed of caring for the garden.',
 dev:'Early counting with real pointing, colour naming (red and black), fine-motor pointing at tiny targets, and the first this-creature-has-a-job idea, planted small.',
 vocab:'ladybug, spots, red, tiny, count, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say ladybug — then let your toddler find it: where is the little red friend sitting?\n2) Hunt the spots together: point to one, then another, saying one, two for as long as the counting mood holds — any total is a triumph.\n3) Draw a big ladybug on paper and let your toddler add the dots with a finger dipped in paint or a fat crayon — the spottier the better.\n4) Tell the helper story in one line: the ladybug eats the tiny bugs that nibble the leaves, so the flowers say thank you.\n5) End with the rule: the ladybug is so small that our big fingers stay far away — we look, we count, and it crawls on its way.',
 say:'"Look — a ladybug! Red and round with little black spots. Can you point to a spot? One... two! The ladybug munches the bugs that nibble the leaves, so the flowers say thank you. We watch our tiny friend and keep our big fingers to ourselves."',
 notice:'Find the ladybug together and say its long, lovely name. This ladybug is red with spots — ask your toddler to point at each spot they can find, and count along for as long as the pointing stays happy.',
 ask:'"Where is the ladybug?" "What colour is it?" "Can you find a spot?" "Do our fingers touch the tiny ladybug, or stay far away?" — answered with points, a colour word and a careful little head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Ladybug!" shouted at anything red and round, spots pointed out on every page from now on, and thumbs painted with dots at craft time. Some toddlers will lower one careful finger towards a real ladybug — the moment to open your palm and redirect to looking.',
 real:'Ladybugs land on leaves, walls and pram hoods all summer. Each one is a rerun: whisper the name, point out the spots you can see, say thank you for helping the flowers, and let it crawl off untouched.',
 play:'Spot-sticker ladybug: draw a big red circle, then hand your toddler black stickers or crayon dots to place wherever spots belong. Peeling and pressing is real fine-motor work wearing a ladybug costume.',
 check:'Show any red-dotted picture tomorrow and ask what it is: a quick ladybug means the word and the shape have bonded. Then ask what ladybugs do all day — munching the leaf-nibblers is the answer worth waiting for.',
 tips:'Count to whatever number the day allows: two spots today, five next week — the pointing matters more than the total. Keep paint dots big and taste-safe, because fingers wander to mouths at two.',
 safety:'A ladybug is fingernail-small, so the looking happens with hands down and eyes close. After any garden or paint session, wash little hands — and nothing from the garden, spots or leaves or bugs, ever goes near a mouth.'},
'snail':{
 title:'Snail',one:'The slowest walker in the garden with its house riding on its back — the snail card teaches slow, slower and slowest, the hardest speed a toddler owns.',
 seoTitle:'Snail Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Snails (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: crawl at snail speed across the rug, meet the shell-house story and learn the after-rain routine — watch snails slowly, then wash little hands.',
 intro:'This lesson is a race to be last: your toddler learns the word snail, crawls across the room at the world’s slowest speed, and meets the garden friend that takes its home along everywhere it goes.',
 what:'A snail is a small, soft creature with a spiral shell on its back that moves — very, very slowly — on one slim foot. Snails come out when the air is damp, after rain or overnight, and munch leaves as they go.',
 mean:'Slow is a genuinely difficult speed for a two-year-old, and the snail makes it a game instead of a command. The shell-house story gives the slowness a reason: the snail carries everything it owns, so of course it takes its time.',
 know:'Snails are slow and carry spiral shells, and damp days bring them out. We watch them without touching — and after any snail encounter, hands go straight to the sink.',
 why:'Crawling slowly builds body control that crawling fast never touches, and the waiting teaches patience with a living example: the snail always arrives, just later. Damp-day snail hunts turn rainy weather into an event.',
 dev:'Slow-motion body control, a first spiral shape found in the wild, weather words (rain, damp, wet), and gentle observation — following one small creature’s whole journey across a step.',
 vocab:'snail, slow, shell, rain, house, watch',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say snail — then ask where its house is: right there on its back, taken along everywhere.\n2) Trace the shell’s round curl on the card with one finger, round and round and in — a spiral is a lovely new shape.\n3) Crawl across the rug at snail speed together: the rule is that nobody may hurry, and the slowest crawler wins the giggle.\n4) After rain, check a wall or a plant pot for a real snail and watch it travel at its own pace — one whole minute is a long, marvellous time.\n5) End with the routine: we looked at the snail, so now we walk inside and wash our hands — snails are watched, never pocketed.',
 say:'"A snail! Slow, slow, slow — see its shell-house riding on its back? It never hurries anywhere. After rain, snails come out for a walk. We watch them crawl, we never touch, and then we wash our hands like big kids."',
 notice:'Say snail together and find the shell — this snail has a brown shell and a smile. Trace the curl with one finger, round and round, and ask whether the snail looks like it is going anywhere in a hurry.',
 ask:'"Where is the snail’s house?" "Can you crawl slowly, slowly?" "When do snails come out?" "Do we touch the snail or watch it?" — answered with a point, a crawl and a slow-motion grin.',
 childSay:'"Slowwww snail!" announced at every pace slower than usual, snails spotted on pavements and flowerpots after rain, and a crawl across the kitchen that takes genuinely forever — the point of the whole game.',
 real:'Rainy days turn every pavement and garden wall into a snail safari. Each find is a rerun: crouch, whisper snail, watch the neck stretch, and leave it exactly where it was heading.',
 play:'Snail race — in reverse: mark a start and a finish cushion, and the winner is whoever takes the longest to cross. Slow motion, silly faces, and the best self-control workout disguised as a race.',
 check:'Say slow out of nowhere tomorrow and watch what happens: a deliberate, exaggerated crawl means the snail left its word behind — the speed and the animal are glued together.',
 tips:'Practise the hand-washing line with a happy voice every single time — the routine matters more than the snail. If rain never comes, a watering can on the same flowerpot makes a fine pretend-damp morning.',
 safety:'Snails trail through soil, leaves and whatever the garden has hosted, so looking is eyes-only and hands get washed straight after garden play. Nothing from the garden — snail, leaf or shell — ever goes near a mouth.'},
'ant':{
 title:'The Ant',one:'Tiny legs, enormous plans — the ant card turns a picnic-crashing insect into a marching game and your toddler into the smallest worker on the line.',
 seoTitle:'Ant Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Ants (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: march like an ant, follow a real ant line with watching eyes only and learn why picnic snacks stay with people and ants stay unbothered.',
 intro:'This lesson finds the smallest card in the set and makes it the busiest: your toddler marches like an ant, follows a real line with their eyes, and learns that ants are always on their way somewhere important.',
 what:'An ant is a very small insect with six legs that walks in long lines with its many friends, carrying crumbs and seeds far bigger than itself. Ants are always going somewhere — usually home.',
 mean:'Ants are the first teamwork a toddler can actually see: a whole line of tiny workers, all heading the same way. The marching game puts your child inside that line, and the watching rule keeps the real workers unbothered.',
 know:'Ants are tiny and march in long lines, and they are heading home with food. We follow a line with our eyes — never fingers — because a bothered ant can nip, and busy workers deserve to get home.',
 why:'Following an ant line with the eyes is real focus training: the line moves, turns and disappears, and your toddler’s attention follows it. The picnic rule teaches the boundary kindly — food is for people, ants have their own plan.',
 dev:'Visual tracking of a moving line, marching and carrying (real heavy-work play), the concept of many-and-small, and early respect for creatures with their own business.',
 vocab:'ant, march, line, tiny, home, watch',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say ant — then hold it low and ask where the tiny ant might be going today.\n2) March like an ant together: small quick steps, arms swinging, maybe carrying a toy crumb — a line of one is still a line.\n3) Lay a trail of toy ants (or buttons) across the rug and follow it, narrating left, right, over, around — the trail is the game.\n4) At a real picnic or on the pavement, find an ant line and watch it with still bodies: where do they all lead? Home, always home.\n5) End with the rule, kindly: ants have their own work, so we watch with our eyes and our snacks stay on our plate.',
 say:'"Look — tiny ants, all in a line! They are carrying their food home, just like you carry your cup. March, march, little ant! When we see real ants, we stand still and watch with our eyes. Busy ants go home; our hands stay up here."',
 notice:'Find the small ant on this card together and say ant. Ask where it might be heading, then march on the spot like a tiny worker — the picture study at ant scale is all in the pointing.',
 ask:'"Where are the ants marching?" "Can you march like an ant?" "Are ants big or tiny?" "Do our fingers chase the ants, or do our eyes?" — answered with marching, the word tiny and pointing fingers.',
 childSay:'"Ants going home!" narrated at every pavement crack, marching lines through the kitchen with teddy crumbs, and ants located on every walk within seconds — the small world now officially open for business.',
 real:'Ant lines cross pavements, garden paths and picnic blankets all summer. Each one is a rerun: crouch, follow with the eyes, whisper where-are-they-going, and leave the line to its work.',
 play:'Ant crumb relay: your toddler carries one small toy crumb from the rug to a basket, over and over, like a worker on the line. Repetitive hauling is exactly what ant bodies do — and exactly what toddler bodies love.',
 check:'Watch the next real ant line: if your toddler crouches to watch instead of stomping, the lesson landed — the eyes took over from the feet, which was always the plan.',
 tips:'Follow one line as far as it goes; the destination matters more than the count. Keep picnic boundaries warm and firm — ants are not chased off, they are simply watched from the blanket.',
 safety:'An ant nips when it is bothered, so the watching is strictly eyes-only with hands folded or held. Food stays with people and gets cleared up after eating — a tidy picnic is the kindest thing anyone can do for ants and toddlers alike.'},
'caterpillar':{
 title:'Caterpillar',one:'Long, green and always hungry — the caterpillar card is the wiggle-and-inch lesson, and the first half of the garden’s best magic trick: one day it becomes a butterfly.',
 seoTitle:'Caterpillar Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Caterpillars (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: inch across the rug like a hungry caterpillar, munch imaginary leaves and hear the garden’s slowest promise — caterpillars grow into butterflies.',
 intro:'This lesson gets low and wiggly: your toddler inches across the rug like a caterpillar, munches through an imaginary garden, and hears the astonishing news that this slow green friend turns into the butterfly card.',
 what:'A caterpillar is a long, soft, many-legged crawler that spends its whole day eating leaves and growing. One day — a bit like magic, but really just growing up — it becomes a butterfly.',
 mean:'The caterpillar is the garden’s appetite champion, and toddlers respect appetite. The inch-and-scrunch movement is genuinely funny to do, and the butterfly promise gives the card a secret that keeps it rewatchable for months.',
 know:'Caterpillars inch along and munch leaves, and every one is a butterfly-in-waiting. They are look-at friends from a little distance — some kinds carry prickly fuzz, so the rule covers them all: eyes only.',
 why:'The inchworm move builds coordination from shoulders to hips in a way nothing else does, and the grow-and-change story is a toddler’s first transformation — the same story they will meet in every butterfly book from now on.',
 dev:'Sequenced movement (reach, scrunch, reach), the munch-and-chew pretending that feeds language, transformation as a concept, and gentle observation of very small travellers.',
 vocab:'caterpillar, inch, munch, leaves, green, butterfly',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say caterpillar — a long word, so stretch it out and let your toddler feel all its syllables.\n2) Get down and inch across the rug together: reach forward with the hands, then tuck the legs in — reach, scrunch, reach, scrunch.\n3) Munch through the garden: each cushion or rug corner is a leaf, and every caterpillar stops to munch it down, bite by bite.\n4) Whisper the secret: this little crawler is growing wings inside its dreams — one day, butterfly. Bring the butterfly card over and let the two meet.\n5) End with the rule: caterpillars are look-at friends from a little way off — fuzz and all, our hands stay back.',
 say:'"A caterpillar! Long and green and always munching. Can you inch the way it does? Reach... scrunch... reach... And guess what — one day this little friend grows wings and becomes a butterfly! We watch caterpillars with our eyes and let them munch."',
 notice:'Say caterpillar together and meet the green crawler on the card. Ask your toddler to trace its long body with one finger from head to tail, then inch two fingers along the card like a tiny crawler.',
 ask:'"What does the caterpillar eat?" "Can you inch like a caterpillar?" "What will it grow into?" "Do we touch the caterpillar, or watch from far away?" — answered with a munch, an inch and a whispered butterfly.',
 childSay:'"Munch munch!" announced at every meal, inchworm races across the living room, and caterpillars inspected solemnly in books with the announcement growing wings — the transformation story already retold in toddler.',
 real:'Caterpillars cruise along leaves, walls and paths in spring and summer. Each find is a rerun: crouch at a polite distance, whisper the name, watch it inch, and leave it to its lunch.',
 play:'Leaf munch trail: lay a trail of green paper leaves across the rug and inch-munch from leaf to leaf, one bite per leaf. Add the butterfly ending: wrap up in a blanket, count to three, and burst out flapping.',
 check:'Ask what a caterpillar becomes: a delighted butterfly is the whole harvest. If the inch-and-scrunch crawl shows up unasked at playtime, the movement landed too — lesson complete.',
 tips:'Join the inching every time; the move is funnier with a grown-up doing it badly. Keep the transformation simple — growing wings inside is plenty of magic for two, no need for the full cocoon science yet.',
 safety:'Some caterpillars carry irritating fuzz that can sting bare skin, so every caterpillar is watched from a little distance, never touched. Hands get washed after garden play, and leaves stay on the plant — not anywhere near a mouth.'},
'dragonfly':{
 title:'Dragonfly',one:'Four shiny wings and the ability to stop mid-air — the dragonfly card is the zoom-freeze-land lesson, and the garden’s own helicopter come to visit.',
 seoTitle:'Dragonfly Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Dragonflies (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: hover, dart and freeze like a dragonfly, land softly on cushion lily pads and learn the pond-side rule — grown-up hands and watching eyes.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches the fastest card at three speeds: your toddler hovers like a dragonfly, darts once, then freezes mid-air — and lands gently on a lily pad, just the way the real flyer does.',
 what:'A dragonfly is a long, thin insect with four glittering wings that zips over ponds and streams, hovers in one spot, then darts off after smaller bugs. It is one of the best flyers in the whole garden.',
 mean:'Hover-dart-freeze is a toddler movement masterpiece: three speeds in one body, ending in the dramatic mid-air stop. And the pond setting makes it the perfect card for practising water-safety rules in play form.',
 know:'Dragonflies zoom and hover near water, and they are look-at friends like everyone in the set. Near any real pond or stream, a grown-up holds the hand — the wings stay in the air game, well away from the water’s edge.',
 why:'The freeze is the secret prize: stopping mid-action is real impulse control, dressed as a dragonfly trick. Pairing it with the pond rule means the quickest card in the set quietly teaches its calmest lesson.',
 dev:'Speed switching (hover, dart, freeze), balance on soft landings, pond-and-water vocabulary, and the safety habit of hand-holding near water, rehearsed long before it is ever needed.',
 vocab:'dragonfly, wings, zoom, freeze, pond, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say dragonfly — then point out the wings: this flyer wears a shiny set of them.\n2) Be a dragonfly together: arms out wide, hover in one spot, then dart two steps sideways — and freeze! Mid-air, mid-word, everywhere.\n3) Spread cushions as lily pads and fly from pad to pad, touching down softly each time — a dragonfly lands like a feather.\n4) Tell the water story simply: real dragonflies live by ponds, and ponds are grown-up-hand places — we visit with fingers linked.\n5) End with three speeds on request: hover... dart... freeze! Then one last feather landing to finish.',
 say:'"Zoom! A dragonfly — four shiny wings! Can you hover? Now dart... and freeze, right in the air, like this! Dragonflies fly over ponds, and ponds mean we hold a grown-up’s hand. Soft wings, soft landings, still feet by the water."',
 notice:'Say dragonfly together and find the shiny wings on the card — this dragonfly has wings that glint. Ask your toddler to flap their own wings once, then make them go still as the card.',
 ask:'"What does the dragonfly do?" "Can you hover and freeze?" "Where do dragonflies fly — over what?" "What do our hands do near a pond?" — answered with a zoom, a freeze and a linked-hand gesture.',
 childSay:'"Zoom, freeze!" played down corridors at top speed and full stop, dragonflies announced at every puddle and pond, and mid-air freezes held with tremendous seriousness — the stop is the trophy move.',
 real:'Dragonflies patrol ponds, canals and damp meadows all summer, often resting on reeds and railings. Each sighting is a rerun: hover-watch quietly, then move on with feet safe and hand held near water.',
 play:'Mid-air statues: dance and dart while a song plays, then freeze like a hovering dragonfly when it stops — wings out, one foot up if brave. The stopping is the game, and the giggles are the reward.',
 check:'Call freeze during ordinary play tomorrow: an instant statue means the dragonfly left its trick behind — self-control arrived on four glittering wings, exactly as designed.',
 tips:'Practise the freeze before the zoom — stopping on cue is the skill worth polishing. Mention the hand-holding rule cheerfully at bath time too; water rules work best when they are rehearsed everywhere.',
 safety:'Real dragonflies live at the water’s edge, so pond and stream visits happen hand-in-hand with a grown-up and never at a run. Dragonflies themselves are harmless — looked at, never caught, and left to their buzzing.'},
'grasshopper':{
 title:'Grasshopper',one:'Crouched low and ready to launch — the grasshopper card is the garden’s jump-start lesson: count down, leap far, land laughing, and measure how far those legs really go.',
 seoTitle:'Grasshopper Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Grasshoppers (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: crouch and jump like the grasshopper on the card, measure leaps heel-to-toe and learn to watch where little feet land in real grass.',
 intro:'This lesson is pure launch power: your toddler crouches like the grasshopper on the card, counts down with you, and jumps — then finds out that grasshoppers leap much farther than their own size, and so can legs that practise.',
 what:'A grasshopper is a green insect with long, strong back legs made for jumping. It hides down in grass and tall plants, then leaps away in one big bound when anything comes near — nobody else in the garden jumps further.',
 mean:'The grasshopper is the set’s jumping card, and jumping is what two-year-old legs dream about. The countdown before each leap adds the best bit: waiting one, two, three makes the jump itself twice as big.',
 know:'Grasshoppers crouch and leap, and they live down in the grass. We jump on rugs and mats, not hard floors — and in real grass we take big careful steps first, because hiding friends do not like being stepped on.',
 why:'Countdown-then-jump builds listening, waiting and explosive movement in one game, and measuring jumps with steps is the gentlest first maths: how far did MY leap go? Bodies learn; numbers sneak in behind.',
 dev:'Powerful crouch-and-jump mechanics, counting down as impulse control, early measuring and comparing, and care for small hidden things — watching the ground as well as the sky.',
 vocab:'grasshopper, jump, crouch, legs, grass, ready',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say grasshopper — then crouch down low yourself, knees bent, ready: your shape teaches first.\n2) Count down together — one, two, three — then jump! Land, laugh, and crouch again for the next launch.\n3) Measure the jumps: heel-to-toe steps from take-off to landing, counted out loud. Grasshoppers leap many times their own length — can you?\n4) Tell the hiding story: grasshoppers live down in the grass, so in real grass we take big careful steps and walk first, jump later.\n5) End with the biggest jump of the day — then a tiny grasshopper crouch, still and quiet, hiding just like the card.',
 say:'"Grasshopper! Look how it crouches... ready, set — JUMP! Grasshoppers have big strong legs and they hide down in the grass. We jump on the rug, and in the garden we watch our feet. One, two, three — launch!"',
 notice:'Say grasshopper together and find the long back legs on the card — this grasshopper is green and ready to jump. Crouch like it, hold for a breath, then do one big jump right there on the rug.',
 ask:'"How does the grasshopper jump — big or small?" "Can you crouch and leap?" "Where does the grasshopper hide?" "In the grass, do we run or watch our feet?" — answered with a crouch, a leap and careful little steps.',
 childSay:'"One, two, three, JUMP!" chanted at sofas, beds and every puddle-adjacent paving stone, jumps measured against grown-up feet all afternoon, and grasshoppers imagined springing from every patch of long grass.',
 real:'Grasshoppers spring out of long grass, meadows and hedge edges all summer — usually seen only as a flash of green. Each flash is a rerun: freeze, whisper grasshopper, watch where it landed, and step carefully.',
 play:'Lily-pad jumping course: cushions or paper pads laid out across the rug, crouch and leap from pad to pad, counting each launch. Land on the pads, not the floor — the aiming is half the exercise.',
 check:'Set up a countdown tomorrow with no card in sight: one, two, three — if a big jump arrives on cue, the legs learned the lesson as well as the ears did.',
 tips:'Jump on soft surfaces only, and jump with your toddler — your leap sets the standard and your landing shows the technique. Keep the measuring silly and short; three heel-to-toe steps is a whole adventure.',
 safety:'All jumping happens on rugs, mats or grass, never tiles or hard floors, and the jump zone gets cleared of furniture first. In real long grass, walkers watch for stones and holes — and for the tiny hidden friends who were there first.'},
'beetle':{
 title:'Beetle',one:'Round, shiny and trundling on six legs — the beetle card is the under-the-pot adventure, and the politest way to learn that every rock is somebody’s roof.',
 seoTitle:'Beetle Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Beetles (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: peek under a lifted flowerpot with a grown-up, meet a shiny beetle on the card first and learn the golden rule — look, then put the roof back.',
 intro:'This lesson goes on the set’s gentlest expedition: your toddler meets the round shiny beetle on the card, then joins a real flowerpot-lift — grown-up hands lifting, toddler eyes discovering, roof back on carefully at the end.',
 what:'A beetle is a small insect with a hard, shiny shell over its wings, and there are more kinds of beetle than almost any other creature. This one trundles along on six legs, tucking under stones, pots and logs by day.',
 mean:'The beetle card is the doorway to the hidden world: the dark, damp addresses under pots and stones where small creatures wait. Your toddler learns that lifting a stone is opening a door — and doors get closed again.',
 know:'Beetles are round and shiny and like dark, damp corners. Only grown-ups do the lifting, everybody looks quickly and kindly, and the stone or pot always goes back — that is somebody’s roof, and roof-back is the whole game.',
 why:'Peek-under-the-pot is genuine discovery science: a guess, a look, and a gentle finish. The put-it-back rule teaches respect for other creatures’ homes long before any pet or nature table arrives.',
 dev:'Turn-taking (grown-up lifts, child looks), observation of small shiny things, the hidden-world idea, and early conservation manners — leaving places exactly as found.',
 vocab:'beetle, shell, shiny, stone, gentle, roof',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say beetle — then trace the round shell with one finger: shiny, smooth, like a little helmet.\n2) Walk like a beetle together: low, slow and steady, arms tucked — trundling is the speed of the day.\n3) Take the card to the garden and choose one flowerpot with your toddler: who might live underneath? Guess first.\n4) Grown-up lifts the pot, toddler looks with still hands: a beetle, a worm, woodlice — hello! Quick look, quiet voices.\n5) Put the roof back gently together, and say the golden line: we always put the house back. That is the beetle’s whole lesson.',
 say:'"A beetle! Round and shiny with a hard little shell. Beetles like dark, cosy places under stones and pots. Shall we look under one? Grown-up lifts, we look with our eyes... and back goes the roof, so our beetle friend stays snug."',
 notice:'Say beetle together, then hunt the card: where is the round, shiny one hiding? Ask your toddler to rub a fingertip gently over the picture to feel how shiny it looks, then trundle two fingers along the card like a little beetle.',
 ask:'"Where does the beetle like to hide?" "Who lifts the pot — you or me?" "What did we see underneath?" "What do we do with the stone afterwards?" — answered with points, a solemn me-or-you and the proud word back.',
 childSay:'"Roof back on!" reported after every pot-lift, beetles located under every rock and log from then on, and trundle-walking adopted as regular playground transport — the hidden world now has a doorman.',
 real:'Beetles trundle through gardens, parks and windowsills all year, most often glimpsed under pots, logs and leaves. Every find is a rerun: look, admire the shine, and lower the roof gently before walking on.',
 play:'Under-the-cup peek: hide a toy beetle under one of two cups and let your toddler lift each one — beetle or no beetle? Small thrills, tidy finish, and every cup goes back exactly where it was.',
 check:'Next garden visit, watch the ending, not the beginning: a pot put back without being asked means the lesson landed — the respect part arrived, which was the hard-won half.',
 tips:'Keep pot-lifting to one or two pots per visit; one good discovery beats a flipped garden. Do the guessing out loud before each lift — imagination does half of the walking to the flowerbed.',
 safety:'Lifting is strictly grown-up work — pots are heavy, edges are rough, and the residents underneath can surprise everyone. After any soil or stone play, hands go to the sink, and nothing from underneath a pot goes anywhere near a mouth.'},
'spider':{
 title:'Spider',one:'Eight wiggly legs and a web of its own making — the spider card turns the garden’s most startling neighbour into a weaving game and the calmest look-only rule of all.',
 seoTitle:'Spider Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Spiders (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: wiggle eight pretend legs, weave a wool web between chairs and learn the spider rule — webs are admired with eyes, and spiders stay in their homes.',
 intro:'This lesson takes the card that makes grown-ups jump and hands it to your toddler as a game: eight legs to wiggle, a web to weave from wool, and one unshakable rule — real spiders are watched with calm eyes and never touched.',
 what:'A spider is a small creature with eight legs that spins a web from its own silky thread. The web catches breakfast, the spider sits and waits, and the whole contraption is rebuilt almost every day.',
 mean:'A spider met on a card first is a spider met calmly: the picture does the introducing, the wool web does the explaining, and by the time a real one appears, watching is already the family habit.',
 know:'Spiders have eight legs and weave webs, and they are strictly look-at friends — the calmest rule in the set. Webs are admired, never poked, and real spiders stay wherever they built their homes.',
 why:'Calm comes from knowing the job: a spider is not lurking, it is working — spinning, waiting, tidying up. A toddler who understands the web sees a worker, not a monster, and the eight-legged fright shrinks to eight-legged interest.',
 dev:'Counting to eight with real wiggles, weaving and threading as fine-motor work, fear met gently through play, and the habit of quiet observation around things that startle.',
 vocab:'spider, web, legs, spin, silk, calm',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say spider — then wiggle eight fingers at each other like eight busy legs: four each, all wiggling.\n2) Count the legs out loud together, one wiggle per leg — eight is a very satisfying number to reach with hands.\n3) Weave a web: loop wool or string across the backs of two chairs, in and over, while your toddler watches and helps hold — then flick pom-pom flies into it for the spider to catch.\n4) Explain the job simply: the spider spins its web, waits, and catches its own dinner — nobody brings it a plate.\n5) End with the rule, calm as anything: real spiders are look-at friends. Webs stay un-poked, spiders stay in their homes, and our hands stay in ours.',
 say:'"A spider! Eight legs — count them with your fingers: wiggle, wiggle, all eight! Spiders spin webs to catch their dinner. When we see a real web, we look with our eyes and keep our hands down. The spider is busy; we are its quiet visitors."',
 notice:'Say spider together and wiggle eight finger-legs. Ask your toddler to find a leg on the card and wiggle just that one — then hold all ten toddler fingers still as a resting spider, which is harder and funnier than it sounds.',
 ask:'"How many legs does the spider have?" "What does it spin?" "Do our fingers touch the web, or do our eyes do the looking?" "Where does the spider live?" — answered with wiggles, a spinning motion and gentle pointing.',
 childSay:'Eight wiggling fingers at the dinner table, wool webs requested daily, and spiders reported calmly from the garden: "spider working!" — the startle replaced by interest, which was the whole quiet plan.',
 real:'Webs appear on hedges, corners and railings, loveliest with morning dew strung across them. Each one is a rerun: crouch, admire the threads, look for the weaver, and walk on without touching a single strand.',
 play:'Web rescue: toss small pom-poms into your wool web, and your toddler plucks them free — careful fingers, one at a time, like a very kind spider tidying up. Unpicking is as absorbing as weaving.',
 check:'Meet a real web tomorrow and watch the hands: still, or reaching? Still hands and a whispered spider mean the calm took — the card did its introducing exactly as planned.',
 tips:'If webs make you jumpy, borrow the calm anyway — your face is the lesson. Keep the wool web up for days; coming back to it daily is what turns a game into understanding.',
 safety:'Some spiders bite when squeezed, and webs stick to little fingers, so real webs and real spiders are admired from a small distance with hands down. The wool-and-chairs web hands your toddler the whole story with zero eight-legged surprises.'},
'moth':{
 title:'Moth',one:'Soft wings on the night shift — the moth card is the garden after dark, a warm porch-light vigil and the gentlest goodnight game in the whole set.',
 seoTitle:'Moth Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Moths (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: flutter softly like the moth on the card, watch the porch light from indoors and meet the garden’s night crew — with a grown-up, window shut.',
 intro:'This lesson opens the garden’s night shift: your toddler learns that some friends wake up at bedtime, flutters around the room on soft brown wings, and watches a lit porch from inside — where the moths come to visit, and no hands go out.',
 what:'A moth is a soft-winged cousin of the butterfly that flies at night. Moths flutter around lamps and porch lights after dark, bump gently along, and rest in the day with their wings spread wide.',
 mean:'The moth introduces night as a working time — the garden has an evening crew. For a two-year-old at bedtime, that idea is delicious: somewhere out there, soft wings are just clocking in.',
 know:'Moths have soft wings and love lamps at night, and they are watch-from-inside friends: the light stays on, the window stays shut, and the fluttering happens with arms, never with hands.',
 why:'Night-watching from a window gives toddlers a safe, thrilling view into the dark — and turns bedtime from an ending into a handover: we sleep, the moths work. The soft-wing flutter also calms a busy body beautifully.',
 dev:'Day-and-night as a first sorting idea, slow gentle movement (the softest flapping in the set), watching patience at a window, and cosy associations that help the light-out transition.',
 vocab:'moth, night, soft, wings, lamp, flutter',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say moth — then stroke your own arm gently: soft, soft, like the wings on the card.\n2) Flutter together: arms as soft wings, bumping gently around the room, landing on cushions — moths are the world’s clumsiest, loveliest flyers.\n3) Tell the night story: when toddlers go to bed, the moths wake up and visit the lamps — the garden has an evening crew.\n4) On a warm evening, stand together at the window with the outside light on and watch: is anyone fluttering out there? Maybe, maybe not — the waiting is the game.\n5) End with the rule, whispered for bedtime: moths are watch-from-inside friends. Wings stay soft, windows stay shut, and our eyes do the visiting.',
 say:'"A moth! Soft little wings — this friend wakes up at night, when we are tucked up. See how it flutters round the lamp? We watch from our window — outside light on, window shut. Flutter, flutter, go the moth wings. Sleep well, little moth!"',
 notice:'Say moth together and find the soft wings on the card — this one is brown and gentle-looking. Ask your toddler to flutter their arms once, then rest them wide like a moth having a daytime sleep.',
 ask:'"When does the moth wake up?" "What does it love — lamps, or soup?" "Can you flutter softly?" "Do we open the window to touch it, or watch from inside?" — answered with a flutter, a giggle and a firm little head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Moth night-night!" reported from the cot, soft-arm flutters adopted as a wind-down move, and moths announced at every lamppost and lit window — the night crew now has a name and a fan club.',
 real:'Moths gather at porch lights, lit windows and lamp posts on warm evenings — visible from indoors better than anywhere. Each flutter is a rerun: lights on, window shut, watch, wave goodnight.',
 play:'Porch-light vigil: five minutes at the window with the outside light on, whisper-counting any flutter that appears. No moths tonight? The game still worked — five quiet minutes of watching is its own bedtime magic.',
 check:'At bedtime, ask who wakes up when we sleep: a confident moth means the day-night idea landed — the garden kept working in your toddler’s imagination all night long.',
 tips:'Keep the window closed and the watching brief; five minutes is the whole show at two. If a moth gets indoors, a grown-up can cup it in a glass and release it outside — narrate the rescue; it is excellent material.',
 safety:'The vigil happens from inside with the window shut, and moths are never caught — fingers rub the dust-fine powder off their wings and squeeze far more than anyone means to. Warm-evening lamp-watching stays on the indoors side, with a grown-up alongside.'},
'cricket':{
 title:'Cricket',one:'You hear it long before you see it — the cricket card is the listening lesson: a summer-evening song, a detective hunt for the musician, and the quietest game in the set.',
 seoTitle:'Cricket Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Crickets (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: go quiet on the doorstep and listen for the cricket’s song, make a chirp of your own and learn that some garden friends are heard, not seen.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches with ears instead of eyes: your toddler hears the cricket’s chirp on the card’s behalf, goes quiet on the doorstep on a warm evening, and plays the set’s best detective game — finding the singer without ever needing to.',
 what:'A cricket is a small hopping insect that sings on summer evenings by rubbing its wings together — chirp, chirp, chirp. The song says the day is cooling down, and the singer hides beautifully in the grass.',
 mean:'The cricket is the set’s sound-first card: the word arrives through the ears, and the eyes may never get a turn. That is a genuine milestone — listening on purpose, with a whole body gone still to help.',
 know:'Crickets chirp on warm evenings and hide in the grass, and finding them is a bonus, never the goal. The game is the listening: ears open, bodies quiet, song enjoyed — the musician stays hidden and unbothered.',
 why:'Deliberate listening is the foundation under every later skill — words, music, reading. The cricket makes it a treasure hunt for sound, and the doorstep version doubles as the gentlest last-game-before-bed in the set.',
 dev:'Focused listening (ears leading, eyes helping), sound imitation, stillness as a listening tool, and evening vocabulary — cool, quiet, song — that softens the whole end of day.',
 vocab:'cricket, chirp, listen, quiet, evening, song',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say cricket — then make the sound together: chirp, chirp, chirp, as fast or slow as your fingers can go.\n2) Play the singer game: one of you closes their eyes while the other chirps from somewhere in the room — can the listener point to the song?\n3) Make a cricket of your own: run a fingertip slowly along a comb, or rub two sticks gently — there, that is the cricket’s own instrument.\n4) On a warm evening, sit together on the doorstep and go very quiet: ears up! Is the cricket singing tonight?\n5) End with the rule of the game: we listen, we whisper, and we let the singer stay hidden — crickets are heard-and-loved friends.',
 say:'"Listen... chirp, chirp, chirp! A cricket is singing somewhere in the grass. Can you hear it? Let’s be very quiet and listen with our ears. Can you make the cricket sound too? The cricket sings all evening — and we listen like little detectives."',
 notice:'Say cricket together and give the card a chirp, chirp. This cricket looks like it is singing — ask your toddler to make the chirping sound back, then go completely still and listen for a pretend answer.',
 ask:'"What does the cricket say?" "Can you hear the song?" "Where is the cricket hiding?" "Do we dig for the singer, or listen?" — answered with chirps, a cupped ear and a whispered hiding-in-the-grass.',
 childSay:'"Chirp chirp!" hummed at bathtime and bedtime, sudden hushes on the doorstep with one finger raised — the family’s new signal for listening — and crickets credited with every evening sound from now on.',
 real:'Warm evenings bring the song to gardens, parks and any grassy verge — heard from prams, windows and doorsteps. Each chirp is a rerun: stop, hush, listen together, and walk on without needing to see the star of the show.',
 play:'Doorstep listening minute: one quiet minute on the step or at the open window, collecting every sound you hear — bird, car, cricket? — whispered back and forth. The cricket version of a bedtime wind-down.',
 check:'On the next warm evening, watch the body when the song starts: if your toddler freezes into their listening pose without being asked, the ears learned the lesson — the quietest victory in the set.',
 tips:'Model the hush yourself; the listening pose is contagious. If the comb is fiddly, two fingers rubbed on a table edge work fine — the point is making the sound, not mastering the tool.',
 safety:'Doorstep and window listening stays with a grown-up and well back from steps and roads. No grass-parting or stone-turning to find the singer — hidden means hidden, and the song comes to you anyway.'},
'roly-poly':{
 title:'Roly-poly',one:'The little grey bug with the built-in again button — the roly-poly card curls into a ball, unrolls, and teaches your toddler the funniest shape in the garden: round.',
 seoTitle:'Roly-poly Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Roly-polies (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: curl into a ball like the roly-poly on the card, unroll slowly and learn the peek-under-the-pot rules — grown-up lifts, gentle eyes, hands washed after.',
 intro:'This lesson rolls with the garden’s smallest gymnast: your toddler curls into a ball and unrolls slowly like the roly-poly on the card, then learns how the real ones get found — gently, under a pot, with the roof going back on.',
 what:'A roly-poly is a small grey creature that rolls into a perfect round ball when it feels shy, then unrolls and trundles off on tiny legs. It lives in damp, dark corners — under pots, stones and leaves.',
 mean:'Curl-and-unroll is the whole lesson in one body move: in, out, in, out. The roly-poly is also the friendliest-looking bug on the card, which makes it the perfect face for the garden’s look-don’t-poke rule.',
 know:'Roly-polies curl into balls and live under pots and stones, and they are look-don’t-poke friends: grown-up lifts, toddler looks, and the roof goes back gently. Hands get washed after every dark-corner adventure.',
 why:'The curling game is real body control — tucking, balancing, holding a shape — and the pot-peeking rules rehearse patience and kindness at the same time. Small creature, enormous manners curriculum.',
 dev:'Body shaping and control (a tight ball is genuinely hard work), gentle curiosity, turn-taking in the peeking game, and the washing-hands routine that follows all soil play.',
 vocab:'roly-poly, curl, ball, roll, gentle, grey',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say roly-poly — it is a funny name, so say it twice and enjoy the wobble of it.\n2) Curl up on the rug into the tiniest ball you can, then unroll slowly, one limb at a time — again! Again!\n3) Roll gently across the rug like a roly-poly trundling home, then curl back up at the far cushion.\n4) Tell the finding story: real roly-polies hide under pots and stones. Grown-up lifts, we peek with quiet eyes, and back goes the roof so our friend stays cosy.\n5) End with the routine: after peeking in dark corners, we wash our hands — and the roly-poly keeps its ball shape for another day.',
 say:'"A roly-poly! Grey and tiny — and when it feels shy, it rolls into a little ball! Can you make yourself tiny and round, like it does? Tuck in tight... now unroll, slowly, slowly. We find them under pots with a grown-up — we look with our eyes and put the roof back."',
 notice:'Say roly-poly together and look for the grey little bug on the card. Curl up small like it, hold the ball for three seconds, then unroll and ask if your toddler can make themselves just as round.',
 ask:'"What does the roly-poly do when it feels shy?" "Can you curl into a ball?" "Who lifts the pot?" "What do we do with our hands afterwards?" — answered with a curl, a point at you and a run to the tap.',
 childSay:'"Again! Again!" between every curl and unroll, roly-polies requested by name at every flowerpot, and ball-shape freezing adopted anywhere a hug of courage is needed — the tiniest card doing the biggest body work.',
 real:'Roly-polies wait under pots, stones and damp leaves all year round. Each find is a rerun: grown-up lifts, one gentle look, a whispered roly-poly, and the roof lowered softly before small feet move on.',
 play:'Curl-and-unroll dice: call curl! and your toddler tucks into a ball; call unroll! and they unwind one arm at a time. Add roly-poly! for a gentle roll across the rug. Three commands, endless repeats.',
 check:'Find a real roly-poly and watch the hands: if they stay down while the eyes go wide, the rule took — and if the pot gets patted goodbye, the kindness took as well.',
 tips:'Keep pot-peeks to one or two per session and let your toddler narrate what they saw afterwards — retelling is the learning. If no real roly-poly turns up, the curling game carries the card perfectly on its own.',
 safety:'Peeking under pots and stones is grown-up-hands work only — fingers stay out from underneath anything lifted. After dark-corner and soil play, hands get washed before snacks, and nothing from the garden goes in mouths.'},
'firefly':{
 title:'Firefly',one:'A tiny light that blinks on and off in the dark — the firefly card is the garden’s lantern, and the quietest magic trick your toddler will ever watch from a window.',
 seoTitle:'Firefly Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Fireflies (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: blink like a firefly on the card, make a paper lantern glow and watch a summer evening from a dark window — the light stays outside, and so do the fireflies.',
 intro:'This lesson is pure glow: your toddler learns the firefly’s blink, plays on-and-off with lights at home, and — if the season and the street allow — watches a dark garden window for the real thing, hands and windows safely still.',
 what:'A firefly is a small beetle that can light up its own body, blinking on and off in the warm dark of summer evenings. The blinking is how fireflies chat with one another, and most toddlers meet them in books first.',
 mean:'The firefly owns the set’s rarest word: glow. On-and-off is a toddler’s favourite game pattern — switches, torches, blinks — and here it arrives attached to a living thing that does it all by itself.',
 know:'Fireflies glow and blink at night, and they are watch-from-the-window friends: catching a firefly switches its light off, so eyes do the admiring and hands stay folded. The blinking games at home use torches and paper, not bugs.',
 why:'Blink-and-freeze games teach watching and waiting in the dark — a big, brave skill at two. The deeper gift is wonder with patience attached: the real light comes on its own schedule, or not at all, and that is part of the magic.',
 dev:'On-off cause and effect, brave-and-calm darkness habits, watching patience at a dark window, and the idea that animals have their own language — light language, in this case.',
 vocab:'firefly, glow, blink, night, light, dark',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say firefly — then blink your hands: fingers open, fingers closed, the firefly’s own on-and-off.\n2) Play blink together in a dim room: light on, light off, whisper glow each time it comes back — the returning light is the joke.\n3) Craft a paper firefly and tuck a small torch or glow dot behind it, then let your toddler work the blinks: on... off... on...\n4) On a real summer night, stand at a dark window or garden step with a grown-up and watch: any tiny lights blinking out there? Quiet eyes, folded hands.\n5) End with the rule, whispered: fireflies are watch-only friends — their light stays on them, and our hands stay in ours.',
 say:'"Look — a firefly! A tiny light in the dark. Blink... blink... it talks with its light! Can you blink like a firefly? At night, we watch from the window with quiet eyes. The firefly keeps its glow, and we keep our hands to ourselves."',
 notice:'Say firefly together and find the light on the card — this firefly glows. Blink your hands open and closed like the firefly’s light, then ask your toddler to make their fingers glow too.',
 ask:'"What does the firefly do at night?" "Can you blink like a firefly?" "Where do we watch from — the window, or the garden?" "Do we catch the light, or look at it?" — answered with blinks, a point to the window and folded hands.',
 childSay:'Hand-blinks in dim rooms at bedtime, fireflies announced at every streetlight and car light from now on, and glow whispered at any small light in the dark — the set’s quietest word, adopted overnight.',
 real:'Fireflies blink over summer lawns, parks and hedgerows in the right season and region — and every torch, phone flash and fairy light makes a fine stand-in the rest of the year. Each blink is a rerun: hush, watch, wave the light goodnight.',
 play:'Firefly torch game: in a dim room, your toddler is the firefly with a small torch — blink on, blink off, while you follow the light with your eyes only. Swap roles: now you glow, and they whisper glow each time.',
 check:'Dim the lights tonight and start blinking: if your toddler joins in with glow on their lips, the card came alive — the light language landed exactly as written.',
 tips:'Keep the dark-watching short, warm and hand-held; bravery grows best in small doses. Any summer-evening garden watching happens with a grown-up, well away from steps, ponds and paths.',
 safety:'Dark-window and garden-step watching is a grown-up-and-toddler affair, hands linked, away from edges and water. Fireflies are never caught — jars and hands switch their lights off for good, so the watching is eyes-only, always.'},
'praying-mantis':{
 title:'Praying mantis',one:'Folded arms, perfect stillness — the praying mantis card is the garden’s patience champion, and the best excuse in the whole set for a very long game of stand-still.',
 seoTitle:'Praying Mantis Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Praying Mantises (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: fold your arms like the mantis on the card, play the longest standing-still game in the garden set and learn why those clever front legs stay far away.',
 intro:'This lesson stands very still and wins: your toddler folds their arms like the mantis on the card, holds the pose one breath longer than seems possible, and meets the garden’s quietest hunter — admired from afar, arms and all.',
 what:'A praying mantis is a tall green insect that stands up on long legs and holds its front arms folded, as if saying hello or thinking hard. It waits without moving for a very long time — then catches its dinner in one quick snap.',
 mean:'The mantis is the stillness card: every other bug teaches a movement, and this one teaches the absence of it. Toddlers adore a challenge, and stand-still-longer-than-you is the set’s finest quiet contest.',
 know:'The mantis stands tall with folded arms and waits patiently, and those clever front legs catch bugs — which is exactly why they stay far away from fingers. Watching a real one means standing just as patiently, a little way back.',
 why:'Holding still on purpose is real self-regulation, and the mantis gives it a hero: a whole animal whose superpower is waiting. The folded arms make the pose copyable, and the quiet makes it a lovely wind-down game.',
 dev:'Stillness and balance as skills in their own right, body imitation (the fold is genuinely tricky), patience with a living model, and the keep-your-distance rule for hunters of any size.',
 vocab:'mantis, still, arms, wait, green, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say praying mantis — then fold your arms up like the card: hello, says the mantis, very politely and very still.\n2) Play the copy game: fold arms, stand tall, hold it... and hold it... until somebody giggles. The giggler folds again.\n3) Try the stillness record: how many breaths can the whole family hold the mantis pose? Count them out loud, slowly.\n4) Tell the waiting story: the mantis stands still because it is waiting for its dinner to walk past — patience is its superpower.\n5) End with the rule, quietly: the mantis catches bugs with those front arms, so our fingers stay far away — we watch, we fold our own arms, and we admire.',
 say:'"A praying mantis! See how it stands — arms folded, perfectly still. Can you stand like it? Fold your arms... shhh... hold it... The mantis waits and waits, then catches its dinner. We watch with quiet eyes and our hands stay way back."',
 notice:'Say praying mantis together and spot the folded arms on the card — this one is green and standing tall. Make the mantis pose yourself, then ask your toddler to hold it while you count slowly to three.',
 ask:'"What are the mantis’s arms doing?" "Can you stand still like the mantis?" "What is it waiting for?" "Do our fingers go near the mantis’s arms, or stay back?" — answered with a fold, a hush and careful little steps backwards.',
 childSay:'Arms folded at the dinner table with tremendous dignity, stillness contests instigated against parents and pets, and mantises announced in every tall plant — the garden’s statue has a fan who now stands just as quietly.',
 real:'Mantises perch on stems, hedges and flower heads, standing so still they are easy to miss and marvellous to find. Each sighting is a rerun: spot it, fold arms, admire the stillness, and step back kindly.',
 play:'Mantis statues: dance gently around the room until someone calls mantis! — then freeze in the folded-arms pose, tallest and stillest wins. Add a slow breath at the end; even statues need to breathe.',
 check:'Call the mantis pose at a random moment tomorrow: a frozen, folded, solemn toddler means the card landed — patience arrived on six long legs, and it can be summoned on request.',
 tips:'Join every contest and lose some on purpose — being beaten at stillness by a two-year-old is the funniest, most motivating prize going. Keep the front-leg rule light; a warning said calmly lands better than one said in alarm.',
 safety:'A mantis catches its food with spiky front arms and one quick snap, so real ones are watched from a respectful distance with hands well back. The folded-arms game stays on rugs and grass — statues and hard floors do not mix.'},
'centipede':{
 title:'Centipede',one:'So many legs, all going at once — the centipede card is the wiggle-count lesson, and the funniest finger-walk your table has ever hosted.',
 seoTitle:'Centipede Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Centipedes (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: walk your fingers like a many-legged centipede, count legs until the giggles win and learn the dark-corner rules — eyes only, hands washed after.',
 intro:'This lesson walks on a hundred make-believe legs: your toddler learns the centipede’s rippling walk with ten wiggly fingers, counts legs until counting collapses into giggles, and meets the quickest mover in the garden’s dark corners.',
 what:'A centipede is a long, flat creature with very, very many legs that ripple along in waves, carrying it surprisingly fast over the ground. It likes dark, damp places and hurries out of the light.',
 mean:'The centipede is the set’s exaggeration card: no toddler will ever count its real legs, and that is the fun — enormous numbers become a joke, a wiggle and a race rather than a lesson.',
 know:'Centipedes have many legs and hurry through dark, damp corners, and they are look-don’t-touch friends like the rest of the set. After any under-the-pot adventure, the sink comes next.',
 why:'Finger-walking builds the little muscles that later hold crayons, and the leg-counting silliness keeps numbers playful exactly when they start getting big. The hurry-away habit also explains something useful: dark corners belong to the creatures in them.',
 dev:'Fine-motor wiggles (ten fingers, independent legs), big-number silliness, observation of fast small movers, and the after-soil hand-washing routine, rehearsed again.',
 vocab:'centipede, legs, wiggle, fast, dark, count',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say centipede — then count a few legs out loud, give up happily, and declare: SO many legs!\n2) Walk your fingers across the table like a centipede — wiggling, rippling, off somewhere in a rush — and let your toddler’s fingers race yours.\n3) Be centipedes on the rug: crawl on hands and knees with extra-fast wiggles, then freeze when the light comes on — centipedes dash for the dark.\n4) Tell the hiding story: centipedes adore dark, damp corners, so under pots and logs is where they hurry home to.\n5) End with the rule: many-legged friends are look-at friends — eyes follow, fingers stay, and hands get washed after every garden adventure.',
 say:'"A centipede! One leg, two legs... SO many legs! See them ripple — wiggle, wiggle, off it goes! Can your fingers walk like a centipede? Fast, fast! Centipedes love dark corners, so we peek with our eyes only — and wash our hands after the garden."',
 notice:'Say centipede together and find the legs on the card — this one is long, with many legs to point at. Count two or three of them, then walk your fingers along the card like a tiny many-legged friend.',
 ask:'"How many legs does the centipede have?" "Can your fingers walk like it?" "Where do centipedes like to hide?" "Do we touch the many-legged friend, or watch?" — answered with wiggles, a big number guess and pointing fingers only.',
 childSay:'Finger-centipedes racing across every table and tummy, absurd leg-counts shouted with total confidence — one hundred! — and centipedes spotted at every log and flowerpot, always off somewhere at speed.',
 real:'Centipedes dash through gardens, under logs and along damp walls, most often seen as a fast ripple and then gone. Each glimpse is a rerun: point, whisper centipede, watch it hurry, and let it get where it was going.',
 play:'Finger-leg races: two centipedes (one hand each, or two for the advanced) race across a table from edge to edge. Fast wiggles, slow wiggles, backwards wiggles — the legs decide everything.',
 check:'Ask tomorrow how many legs a centipede has: any enormous, delighted number is a perfect pass — the exaggeration landed, and so did the wiggle that carries it.',
 tips:'Keep the counting brief and the wiggle long; the race teaches more than the arithmetic at this age. If a real centipede startles you, borrow its own lesson — freeze, breathe, and narrate it hurrying home.',
 safety:'Centipedes hurry away rather than hug, but fingers stay out of dark corners all the same — pot-lifting is grown-up work, and peeking is eyes-only. Garden play ends at the sink: hands washed, snacks after, nothing from the garden in mouths.'},
'earthworm':{
 title:'Earthworm',one:'Pink, wiggly and quietly the garden’s most important worker — the earthworm card teaches the softest wiggle, the kindest rain rule and why the gardener underground never gets touched.',
 seoTitle:'Earthworm Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Earthworms (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: wiggle like an earthworm, roll playdough worms long and longer and learn the rain-day rule — worms come up for air, and we watch with dry, kind hands.',
 intro:'This lesson honours the garden’s hidden hero: your toddler wiggles like the pink earthworm on the card, rolls worms out of playdough, and learns why the busiest hands in the soil are the ones we never touch.',
 what:'An earthworm is a long, soft, pink creature that lives in the soil, wiggling through it all day and turning old leaves into food for plants. No worm, no garden — they are the quiet workers beneath every bloom your toddler has ever sniffed.',
 mean:'The earthworm is the set’s least glamorous card and its deepest lesson: the most important worker is the one nobody sees. For a two-year-old, the worm is also the perfect wiggle — long, slow, and impossible to do standing up.',
 know:'Worms wiggle underground and come up after rain, and they are never-touch friends: their skin does their breathing, and dry hands would hurt it. We watch with kind eyes, and if one is stranded on the path, a grown-up moves it with a leaf.',
 why:'Caring for a creature too small and soft to defend itself is kindness at its purest — and the reason is touchable: worm skin breathes, so our dry hands are the danger. Rules with real reasons stick for life.',
 dev:'Long, slow body wiggles (surprisingly hard, endlessly funny), the underground-world idea, gentleness practised at its most delicate, and rain-day observation habits.',
 vocab:'earthworm, wiggle, soil, rain, soft, kind',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say earthworm — then wiggle: arms loose, shoulders loose, everything wiggling like something with no bones at all.\n2) Lie on the rug and do the worm: stretch long, pull in short, stretch long again — travelling by wiggles only.\n3) Roll playdough worms together — long, longer, the longest worm in the garden — and line them up for a wiggle parade.\n4) After rain, look for a real worm on a path or lawn and watch how it reaches and bunches, stretch by stretch: it came up for air, like a little swimmer.\n5) End with the rule, softly: a worm’s skin does its breathing, so our dry hands stay far away — we watch, we wiggle like them, and we leave them to their rain.',
 say:'"An earthworm! Pink and wiggly — it lives under the ground and helps the flowers grow. Wiggle, wiggle! After rain it comes up to breathe. We watch with our kind eyes and never touch — our hands are too dry, and the worm is very soft."',
 notice:'Say earthworm together and look for the pink wiggler on the card. Ask your toddler to wiggle like the worm, then trace its long body with one finger from end to end and back again.',
 ask:'"Where does the worm live?" "When does it come up — after what?" "Can you wiggle like a worm?" "Do our hands touch the worm, or stay dry and far away?" — answered with wiggles, the word rain and a gentle head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Wiggle worm!" announced at bathtime and every rainy window, playdough worms coiled all over the kitchen table, and worms rescued-in-story after every shower of rain — the underground hero has a spokesman.',
 real:'Rainy days bring worms up onto paths and lawns, and every one is a rerun: crouch, whisper earthworm, watch it stretch, and leave it to its air. Dry-weather digging turns up another — admired, then left to its tunnel.',
 play:'Playdough worm families: roll a mummy worm, a baby worm and the longest worm of all, then measure them against each other and against a real finger. Quiet hands, soft dough, and the worm’s whole shape lesson in one sitting.',
 check:'Next rainy day, watch the crouch: if your toddler leans in to look with hands clasped behind their back, the rule grew roots — kindness and knowing, exactly as planted.',
 tips:'The never-touch rule needs its reason said out loud at least once: worm skin breathes, and our dry hands would hurt it. Reasons turn rules into values — and this one is true.',
 safety:'Worms are watched, never handled — even gently, dry fingers damage their skin. After rain or soil play, hands get washed before anything touches a face or snack, and nothing from the garden goes in mouths, ever.'},
'bird':{
 title:'Bird',one:'A flash of blue on a branch — the bird card is the garden’s loudest regular, and the lesson that turns every window into a watching post and every tweet into a name.',
 seoTitle:'Bird Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Birds (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: tweet like the bluebird on the card, sprinkle seeds and stand back, and learn the watching rule — birds come to stillness, never to chasing.',
 intro:'This lesson looks up: your toddler learns the word bird, makes the tweet, and meets the garden’s fastest visitor — the friend that only comes close when everyone gets very quiet and very still.',
 what:'A bird is an animal with feathers and wings that flies, hops and sings. This one is a bluebird perched on a branch — and birds like it visit gardens, hedges and windowsills every single morning, hunting seeds and crumbs.',
 mean:'Birds are the only wild animals most toddlers see every day, doing exactly what toddlers love: appearing, moving, disappearing. Naming the bird turns the window into a daily show with a returning cast.',
 know:'Birds tweet, fly and hop, and they come to stillness: running feet send them off, quiet feet bring them near. We watch birds and never chase — the stiller the watcher, the longer the show.',
 why:'Bird-watching is patience with a payoff — the child sees, in minutes, that quiet bodies are rewarded. That cause and effect is learned in the body, and it transfers to every library, queue and waiting room to come.',
 dev:'Stillness as a real skill, sound imitation, visual tracking of the quickest thing in the garden, and the gentle beginnings of caring about wild things that need nothing from us.',
 vocab:'bird, tweet, wings, seeds, quiet, fly',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say bird — then tweet together, two little songs, so the word arrives with its sound.\n2) Find the bird’s parts on the card: the wing, the beak, the tail, the branch it sits on — pointing is how two-year-olds read a picture.\n3) Take the card to the window and whisper: is a bird out there right now? Look quietly. A pigeon counts, a crow counts, everything with wings counts.\n4) Sprinkle a few seeds on a sill or step, step back, and hold the stillness together — count the quiet seconds out loud, softly.\n5) End with the rule: birds are watch-friends — quiet feet, down-held hands, and they come close all by themselves.',
 say:'"Listen — tweet tweet! A bird! Birds have wings and they fly, and they hop along the fence. Let’s be quiet as mice and watch the window... still feet, soft voices... maybe a bird will come and say hello. Birds are our look-at friends!"',
 notice:'Say bird together and point out the bluebird — on this card it sits on a branch. Point to the branch, then the wing, then tweet twice, softly, so the card stays a window game and not a shout.',
 ask:'"What does the bird say?" "Where is the bird sitting?" "Can you make your feet go still?" "Do we chase the bird, or watch it?" — answered with a tweet, a point and feet that stop on request.',
 childSay:'"Bird!" shouted at full outdoor volume and whispered at indoor windows, tweets exchanged over breakfast, and every pigeon, crow and duck promoted to the same beloved word — the sky is officially inhabited now.',
 real:'Birds are the most reliable show in any street: fences, hedges, car parks, washing lines. Each visit is a free rerun — point, whisper, watch, wave goodbye — and the seeds keep the cast coming back.',
 play:'Seed sprinkle and freeze: shake a tiny cup of seeds onto a step or sill, then hold hands and play freeze — crouch, breathe loud enough to hear, wait. The prize for winning is usually a bird.',
 check:'At the next window, watch the feet and the voice: still feet and a whisper mean the lesson landed — your toddler has traded chasing for watching, which is the whole trade this card offers.',
 tips:'Keep a card and a seed cup by the window permanently; the ritual runs itself within a week. Two minutes is a full sitting at this age — stop while the pointing is still happy.',
 safety:'Windows and doors stay shut during bird-watching, and sills are for seeds, not elbows. Feed birds outside where they stay wild, wash little hands after touching seed, and let the chasing happen only in games.'},
'frog':{
 title:'Frog',one:'Squat, leap, splash — the frog card is the garden’s jump champion, and the pond-side lesson that some friends are loved best from the bank, hands in pockets.',
 seoTitle:'Frog Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Frogs (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: crouch and leap to cushion lily pads, ribbit between jumps and learn the pond rule — frogs are watched from dry land, and never picked up.',
 intro:'This lesson turns your rug into a pond: your toddler learns the frog’s crouch and leap, ribbits between landings, and absorbs the set’s water rule — real frogs live at ponds, and ponds are grown-up-hand places.',
 what:'A frog is a small, green, long-legged animal that sits still by the water and then disappears in a single huge leap. It catches bugs with a quick flick of its tongue and says — everyone together — ribbit.',
 mean:'The frog is the set’s movement finale: crouch, launch, land, repeat. And because real frogs live at the water’s edge, the card doubles as the gentlest water-safety lesson in the garden.',
 know:'Frogs crouch and leap and say ribbit, and they live by ponds. We look at frogs from the bank — their damp skin is far too delicate for warm, dry hands — and any real pond means a grown-up holds on.',
 why:'Jumping builds the big power skills — crouch depth, launch, controlled landing — while the pond rule rehearses respect for wild skin and water. One card, two lessons, both learned in the legs.',
 dev:'Explosive jumping and quiet landing, listening-freeze control, amphibian vocabulary, and water-safety habits rehearsed long before any real pond is in sight.',
 vocab:'frog, jump, ribbit, pond, lily pad, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say frog — then crouch low yourself, hands between your knees: the shape teaches before the word lands.\n2) Do one enormous jump together and land with a soft plop, then say ribbit! Word, jump and sound in one event.\n3) Scatter cushions as lily pads and leap from pad to pad, ribbiting on every landing — the quieter the landing, the better the frog.\n4) Tell the pond story simply: real frogs sit by the water, and we visit ponds holding a grown-up’s hand — we look from the bank, and our hands stay in ours.\n5) Finish with the still frog: crouch very small and freeze — then one last giant leap to end the session on a laugh.',
 say:'"A frog! Ribbit, ribbit! Frogs crouch down low... and JUMP! Can you leap to the lily pad? Frogs live by the pond — when we visit, we hold hands and watch from the bank. We never pick frogs up; their skin is soft and ours is too dry. Ribbit!"',
 notice:'Say frog together and spot the green one waiting on the card. Crouch like the frog, hold for one breath, then leap once on the spot and give your biggest ribbit as you land.',
 ask:'"What does the frog say?" "Can you leap like a frog?" "Where do frogs live?" "Do we touch the frog at the pond, or watch from the bank?" — answered with a ribbit, a leap and a solemn linked-hands gesture.',
 childSay:'"Ribbit!" punctuating bath time, cushion ponds rebuilt daily across the living room, and frogs announced at every puddle and pond on every walk — with feet stopping at the bank, which is the rule arriving.',
 real:'Frogs appear at pond edges, damp gardens and after-rain paths — mostly as a flash of green and a splash. Each flash is a rerun: freeze, whisper frog, watch the jump, and keep feet on dry land.',
 play:'Lily-pad hop course: cushions spaced across the rug, frog crouch between each leap, silent-landing points awarded generously. End as every good frog does — one enormous final jump into your arms.',
 check:'Say ribbit out of nowhere tomorrow: if a crouch-jump arrives before the laugh does, the word lives in the legs — the pond rule usually arrives in the same package, so listen for the bank part too.',
 tips:'Join every leap; your landing quality sets the standard. Keep pond talk warm and frequent — baths, paddling pools, puddles — because water rules work best when they are rehearsed everywhere, not just at ponds.',
 safety:'Real ponds, streams and even deep puddles are grown-up-hand territory, always. Frogs are never picked up — their damp skin is delicate and would dry out on ours — so the leaping stays on cushions and the watching stays on the bank.'},
'squirrel':{
 title:'Squirrel',one:'Twitchy tail, tree-top dashes and buried treasure — the squirrel card closes the garden with its quickest performer, and a freeze-and-watch game your toddler will play on every walk.',
 seoTitle:'Squirrel Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Squirrels (Class 14)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: scurry and freeze like the squirrel on the card, hunt buried nuts under cups and learn the watching rule — squirrels are admired from the path, never fed or chased.',
 intro:'This lesson borrows the garden’s quickest game: your toddler scurries and freezes like the squirrel on the card, plays the nut-hiding trick that real squirrels play all winter, and learns to watch the park’s best show from the path.',
 what:'A squirrel is a quick, bushy-tailed animal that runs up trees, along fences and across lawns, burying nuts to find again later. Its tail flicks and twitches — and it is almost always the first wild animal a toddler meets doing something dramatic.',
 mean:'Squirrels close the set with the game toddlers were born for: dash, stop, look. The nut-burying story gives the dash a plot, and the tail gives the whole animal a flag your child will spot from a pram at fifty paces.',
 know:'Squirrels scurry, freeze and flick their fluffy tails, and they are watch-from-the-path friends: wild things stay wild, so we never chase, touch or feed them — admiring is the better gift.',
 why:'Freeze-and-watch is attention training disguised as the squirrel’s own trick, and spotting one in a tree is a genuine search-and-find triumph. Owning an animal seen every day builds the confident feeling that the world is knowable.',
 dev:'Visual tracking at top speed, impulse control (the freeze), pretend play with a plot (bury, remember, dig), and respect for wild animals met on their own terms.',
 vocab:'squirrel, tail, tree, nuts, scurry, watch',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say squirrel — then wag an arm-tail behind you: the make-believe tail makes the word unforgettable.\n2) Play the squirrel’s own game: scurry three steps, freeze, look left, look right, scurry again — narrating every move as you both do it.\n3) Hide a toy nut under one of three cups, shuffle slowly, and let your squirrel find it — then swap: you hunt, they hide.\n4) Tell the winter story: squirrels bury nuts everywhere and come back for them later — some are forgotten, and those grow into trees.\n5) End with the rule, on the move: squirrels are watch-from-the-path friends — eyes follow, feet stay with the grown-up, and hands stay full of nothing.',
 say:'"A squirrel! See the big fluffy tail — twitch, twitch! Squirrels run up trees and bury their nuts for winter. Can you scurry like a squirrel? Now freeze — and look around! When we see a real one, we stand on the path and watch its show. No chasing, no feeding — just watching."',
 notice:'Say squirrel together and find the tail on the card — this one has a big fluffy tail. Wag your arm-tail while you ask what the tail is doing, then scurry-and-freeze once across the rug.',
 ask:'"What is the squirrel’s tail doing?" "Where does the squirrel run — up what?" "Where did it put the nuts?" "Do we chase the squirrel, or watch from the path?" — answered with a wag, a point upwards and still little feet.',
 childSay:'"Squirrel!" announced at top volume in every park and garden, arm-tails wagged at dinner, nut-hiding games running under sofas all winter — and the first honest freeze when a real one dashes past, the watching rule working exactly as built.',
 real:'Squirrels perform daily wherever trees grow: gardens, parks, churchyards, the tree outside the bakery. Every dash is a rerun — point, whisper, follow with your eyes, and keep your feet on the path.',
 play:'Three-cup squirrel stash: hide a nut or a small toy under one cup, shuffle, and let your squirrel remember where it went. Squirrels lose a few nuts every winter too — losing the round is part of the joke.',
 check:'On the next walk, watch the feet when a real squirrel appears: still, or running? Still feet and a pointing finger mean the lesson landed — the freeze replaced the chase, and the show got a respectful audience.',
 tips:'Say eyes follow, feet stay on every sighting until your toddler finishes the line for you. Keep the cup game tiny — three cups, one treasure — and let your child be the hider half the time; hiding is the cleverer job.',
 safety:'Squirrels are wild things with tiny sharp teeth and quicker claws, so watching happens from the path with hands down — no chasing, no offering food, and no collecting acorns or conkers for anything but the toy game. Hands get washed after conker play, and nothing from the park goes in mouths.'}
};
